-- Q4299: Entity Choice and Planning [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Entirely tax-free as a return of shareholder basis","$150,000 dividend from E&P, then $50,000 basis return","AAA first (tax-free to basis), then E&P dividend, then basis return","Entirely treated as a taxable dividend to the shareholder"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Sec. 1368(c), distributions from an S corporation with accumulated E&P follow a layered ordering: first from the accumulated adjustments account (AAA) tax-free to the extent of stock basis, then from accumulated E&P as dividends under Sec. 301(c)(1), then as return of remaining basis.
Wrong (A): The distribution cannot be entirely tax-free because the corporation has $150,000 of accumulated E&P from its C corporation years that must be distributed as dividends.
Wrong (B): This skips the AAA layer; S corporation current earnings in AAA are distributed first before reaching the E&P layer.
Wrong (D): Only the E&P portion is a dividend; the AAA and basis return portions receive different treatment.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4299;

-- Q4309: Entity Choice and Planning [hard/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The conversion qualifies as a tax-free reorganization","There are no tax consequences to the corporation or shareholders","The shareholders receive a stepped-up basis in partnership interests","The corporation recognizes gain on appreciated assets at FMV upon liquidation"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Converting a C corporation to a partnership is treated as a complete liquidation. Under Section 336, the corporation must recognize gain on all appreciated assets as if sold at FMV. Shareholders also recognize gain under Section 331 to the extent distributions exceed stock basis.
Wrong (A): A C-to-partnership conversion is not a tax-free reorganization; it requires corporate liquidation triggering gain recognition.
Wrong (B): Significant tax consequences arise at both the corporate and shareholder levels upon liquidation.
Wrong (C): Shareholders receive a fair market value basis in partnership interests, but this does not eliminate the gain recognized on liquidation.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4309;

-- Q4312: Entity Choice and Planning [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
