  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Sec. 1361-1379, an S corporation allows owners to split income between reasonable compensation subject to FICA (Sec. 3101-3111) and pass-through distributions exempt from self-employment tax, providing significant payroll tax savings on the distribution portion.
Wrong (A): A C corporation paying only salaries still faces payroll taxes on all compensation with no distribution-based savings.
Wrong (B): Retaining all C corporation earnings avoids dividends but creates double taxation risk and potential accumulated earnings tax under Sec. 531.
Wrong (C): In a general partnership, all ordinary business income is subject to self-employment tax under Sec. 1402(a), resulting in a higher overall tax burden.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4312;

-- Q4313: Entity Choice and Planning [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Deduct losses exceeding stock basis up to the loan amount","Avoid recognizing income on S corporation distributions","Receive tax-free interest payments on the shareholder loan","Claim an immediate bad debt deduction for the loan"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under Section 1366(d)(1)(A), direct shareholder loans create debt basis in an S corporation. The shareholder can deduct losses first against stock basis, then against debt basis up to the loan amount. Subsequent income restores debt basis before stock basis.
Wrong (B): Debt basis does not allow a shareholder to avoid income recognition on distributions; distribution treatment is governed by stock basis under Sec. 1368.
Wrong (C): Interest received on a shareholder loan is taxable ordinary income, not tax-free.
Wrong (D): A bad debt deduction requires the loan to become worthless; merely making the loan does not create an immediate deduction.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4313;

