-- Q4354: Nontaxable Dispositions [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Same asset class as the destroyed property","Identical property matching the original exactly","Like-kind property under the Sec. 1031 standard","Similar or related in service or use to the original"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Sec. 1033(a), involuntary conversions other than condemnations require the replacement property to be similar or related in service or use to the converted property. This functional use test is stricter than the like-kind standard, requiring the taxpayer to use the replacement in a similar manner.
Wrong (A): The IRS does not use an asset class standard for involuntary conversions; the test focuses on functional similarity.
Wrong (B): An identical property standard would be too restrictive; Sec. 1033 requires similar use, not an exact match.
Wrong (C): The like-kind standard under Sec. 1031 applies to voluntary exchanges and condemnations of real property under Sec. 1033(g), but not to casualty conversions.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4354;

-- Q4365: Nontaxable Dispositions [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["No gain is recognized because gifts are nontaxable events","Donor recognizes gain: FMV of obligation minus donor''s basis","The donee assumes all deferred gain and reports it as received","The installment obligation is deemed cancelled upon the gift"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under Section 453B(a), disposing of an installment obligation by gift triggers gain recognition. The donor recognizes gain equal to the difference between the FMV of the obligation and the donor''s basis in it, ensuring the deferred gain is not permanently avoided.
Wrong (A): Although gifts are generally nontaxable to the donee, Sec. 453B(a) specifically treats any disposition of an installment obligation—including a gift—as a gain recognition event for the donor.
Wrong (C): The donee does not assume the deferred gain; the donor must recognize it at the time of the gift.
Wrong (D): A gift does not cancel the obligation; the note remains enforceable, but the donor must recognize gain on the disposition.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4365;

