-- Q4383: Related Party Transactions [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Fully deductible in the current tax year","Disallowed and added to the basis of replacement shares","Permanently lost with no future tax benefit","Deductible only in the following tax year"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under Section 1091, a loss is disallowed when substantially identical securities are acquired within 30 days before or after the sale. The January 5 repurchase falls within 30 days. The disallowed $10,000 loss is added to the basis of the new shares, preserving it for future recognition.
Wrong (A): The loss cannot be deducted currently because the wash sale rule applies when replacement shares are bought within the 61-day window.
Wrong (C): The loss is not permanently lost; it is added to the basis of the replacement shares and recognized when those shares are eventually sold.
Wrong (D): The wash sale rule does not merely defer the deduction to the next year; it adjusts the basis of the new shares instead.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4383;

-- Q4384: Related Party Transactions [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Only the 30 days after the sale date","Only the 30 days before the sale date","30 days before and 30 days after the sale (61-day window)","The full 60 days following the sale date"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under Section 1091, the wash sale period spans 30 days before through 30 days after the sale, creating a total 61-day window. Acquiring substantially identical securities at any point in this window disallows the loss.
Wrong (A): The window is not limited to the period after the sale; purchases before the sale also trigger the rule.
Wrong (B): The window is not limited to the period before the sale; purchases after the sale also trigger the rule.
Wrong (D): The post-sale window is 30 days, not 60 days, and the rule also covers 30 days before the sale.$EXPL$
